question.:question/worked-solution:
Use the scalar line-integral formula

$$
\int_C f\,\mathrm{d}s=\int_a^b f(\mathbf r(t))\,\|\mathbf r'(t)\|\,\mathrm{d}t.
$$

Differentiate both coordinates and compute the speed:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf r'(t)&=\langle -2, -2\rangle \\
\|\mathbf r'(t)\|&=\sqrt{(-2)^2+(-2)^2} \\
&=2 \sqrt{2}
\end{aligned}
$$



Substitute the coordinates into the density:

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(\mathbf r(t))&=\frac{1}{(- 2 t - 4)-(- 2 t)} \\
&=\frac{1}{-4}=- \frac{1}{4}
\end{aligned}
$$

Multiply by the speed and integrate, subtracting the lower endpoint:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_C f\,\mathrm{d}s&=\int_{1}^{4} \left(- \frac{1}{4}\right)\cdot (2 \sqrt{2})\,\mathrm{d}t \\
&=\int_{1}^{4} - \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\,\mathrm{d}t \\
&=\left[- \frac{\sqrt{2} t}{2}\right]_{1}^{4} \\
&=(- 2 \sqrt{2})-\left(- \frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\right) \\
&=- \frac{3 \sqrt{2}}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$
